  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 9f3b431661c0ec24c86026f9b9353301, type: 3}
  m_Name: 4.HowToUse
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::Readme
  icon: {fileID: 0}
  title: How to Use
  sections:
  - heading: How to Use
    text: "How to Use\n\nHow to Control the Bar\n\nControlling the bar is simple
      and works the same for both UI and 3D bars. The ProceduralProgressBar script
      provides a few key methods:\n\nBasic API Usage\n\ncsharp\n// Get a reference
      to the progress bar component\nProceduralProgressBar bar = GetComponent();\n\n//
      Instantly set the bar fill (no animation)\nbar.UpdateBarFillAmount(0.75f);\n\n//
      Smoothly fill the bar by a specific amount (uses default fill duration)\nbar.BarFill(0.3f);
      // Fill 30%\n\n// Smoothly fill the bar by a specific amount over custom time\nbar.BarFill(0.2f,
      0.5f); // Fill 20% over 0.5 seconds\n\n// Smoothly reduce the bar value (uses
      default loss duration)\nbar.BarLoss(0.3f); // Reduce 30%\n\n// Smoothly reduce
      the bar value over custom time\nbar.BarLoss(0.2f, 0.3f); // Reduce 20% over
      0.3 seconds\n\nReal-World Example\n\nTo see these methods in action:\n\u2022
      Open the script usage example (not included in this checkout)\n\u2022 Check
      the BarsManager.cs script at:\\\n  Assets/ThirdParty/CupOHappiness/ProceduralProgressBars/`Runtime/ProceduralProgressBar.cs`\n\nThis
      script includes comments and examples for integrating bars with gameplay logic.\n[[IMAGE:0]]\n\nTip:
      Use Initial Fill Amount\\\nSet a starting fill value that will be applied automatically
      when the scene begins. This is useful for visually initializing the bar without
      writing any custom scripts.\n\u2022 Bar Look Customization\n[[IMAGE:1]]\n\nAll
      bar visuals are controlled through the Material Shader Properties. You can
      change roundness, colors, gradients, outlines, shadows, segment styles, and
      more.\n\nWhere to Start\n\u2022 Use the Features Showcase.unity to preview
      each shader feature individually.\n\u2022 Explore the Styles Showcase.unity
      for polished, ready-to-use bars.\n[[IMAGE:2]]\n\nCheck the Shader Properties
      for explanations of every option.\n\nHow to Edit\n\u2022 Select the bar\u2019s
      Material.\n\u2022 Close tabs you don\u2019t need to keep the UI clean.\n\u2022
      Adjust any property directly in the Inspector.\n\nNote: Some features must
      be enabled with a checkbox (e.g. \"Enable Vertical Darken\"). Disabled features
      are removed from the shader code at runtime for better performance.\n\u2022
      Bar Animation Customization\n[[IMAGE:3]]\n\nThe bar supports two animation
      stages: Fill (when increasing) and Loss (when decreasing). Each stage has its
      own configurable behavior.\n\nWhere to Start\n\u2022 Use the script usage example
      (not included in this checkout) to preview all of the bar animation features
      and settings. \n\nUsing the Config System\n[[IMAGE:4]]\n[[IMAGE:5]]\n\nYou
      can save and load your bar\u2019s visual and animation settings using a Config
      file.\n\u2022 In Editor Mode, assign or create a config in the \u201CConfig\u201D
      field.\n\u2022 You can click \u201CCreate New\u201D to generate a new config
      file.\n\u2022 Hit 'Play Mode' and tweak your settings, click \u201CSave To
      Config\u201D to save your changes.\n\u2022 After exiting Play Mode, click \u201CLoad
      From Config\u201D to reapply the saved changes.\n\nImportant: Unity discards
      all scripts (NOT material changes - changes you made to the bar material are
      safe) made during Play Mode unless they\u2019re saved. Make sure to \"Save
      To Config\" script settings during runtime, and use the \u201CLoad From Config\u201D
      button after stopping the game to bring back your saved setup.\n\nConfigs store
      values from the Fill, Loss, Impulse, Second Bar, and Auto End Line tabs. They
      do not store material assignments, Setup tab options or any material properties
      settings.\n\nThis workflow lets you preview and test bar animations quickly
      during runtime, then keep your changes without manually reapplying them each
      time after you stop runtime.\n\nTesting Buttons\n\nUnder the \u201CSetting
      Up (only in runtime)\u201D section in Play Mode, you\u2019ll find:\n\u2022
      Bar Fill Amount Editor \u2014 enter a value (e.g. 0.25) and click Bar Fill
      to test a fill animation.\n\u2022 Bar Loss Amount Editor \u2014 enter a value
      and click Bar Loss to test a loss animation.\n\nThese tools work the same way
      as calling BarFill() or BarLoss() in your scripts, letting you preview how
      your animations and effects behave during runtime.\n\nAnimation Customization
      Tabs\n\nYou\u2019ll find four main tabs in the ProceduralProgressBar script:\n\n1.
      Fill Settings \u2013 Controls how the bar fills, including speed and animation
      curve.\n2. Loss Settings \u2013 Controls how the bar loses value, also with
      its own curve and speed.\n3. Impulse Settings \u2013 Adds visual effects like
      flashes or shadows when the bar changes.\n4. Second Bar Settings \u2013 Enables
      a secondary bar for effects like ghost HP or delayed visuals.\n\nEach section
      contains properties you can change and test during runtime. You can see examples
      of these settings in action in the script usage example (not included in this
      checkout).\n\nExample configs from that scene can be found here: Assets/ThirdParty/CupOHappiness/ProceduralProgressBars/Demo
      Files/Settings\n[[IMAGE:6]]\n\nThese configs can be reused, modified, or saved
      again with your own adjustments.\n\nFill Settings\n\u2022 Default Fill Time
      and Default Loss Time are used when you don\u2019t provide a custom duration
      in BarFill() or BarLoss().\n\u2022 Animation Curves let you control easing/speed
      for each stage.\n[[IMAGE:7]]\n\ncsharp\n// Uses default fill time\nbar.BarFill(0.3f);\n\n//
      Overrides default with 0.2 seconds\nbar.BarFill(0.3f, 0.2f);\n\nAuto End Line
      Settings\n\nWhen enabled, the End Line automatically appears or disappears
      based on fill level.\n\u2022 Use it to hide the end marker when the bar is
      nearly full.\n\u2022 Set a threshold value, and define how long it takes to
      appear/disappear.\n\u2022 Make sure the end line feature is enabled in your
      material.\n\u2022 Turn Off/On Time \u2013 Time of the end line animation.\n\u2022
      Fill Amount Threshold \u2013 When the bar is above this value, the end line
      hides.\n[[IMAGE:8]]\n[[IMAGE:9]]\n\nSecond Bar Settings\n\nThis adds a second
      fill layer behind the main bar. It\u2019s ideal for things like:\n\u2022 Delayed
      fill/loss (e.g., ghost HP)\n\u2022 Visual contrast between real-time and buffered
      values\n[[IMAGE:10]]\n\nRequires \"Enable Second Bar\" to be turned on in the
      material.\n\nBoth stages have options:\n\u2022 Use Time Delay \u2013 Adds a
      pause before the second bar animates.\n\u2022 Catch-Up Time \u2013 Controls
      how fast the second bar \"catches up\" to the main one.\n\u2022 Override Color
      \u2013 Lets you tint the second bar differently.\n\u2022 Blend to Main (fill)
      / Invisible (loss) \u2013 Smoothly fades second bar toward main bar or hides
      it after delay.\n\nImpulse Settings\n\nAdds dynamic visual feedback when bar
      value changes (e.g. flashes, glow, motion).\n\nImpulse Curve & Duration \u2013
      How the impulse animates over time.\n\nEach impulse type supports:\n\u2022
      Custom Outline \u2013 Animate outline strength or color.\n\u2022 Fill Shadow
      \u2013 Animate soft shadows inside the bar.\n\u2022 Overlay \u2013 Animate
      glow or brightening effects over the bar surface.\n\nImpulse features require
      their counterparts to be enabled in the shader (e.g., Custom Outline, Fill
      Shadow, Overlay). \n\u2022 \u2022\n[[IMAGE:11]]"
